%S A305609 0,1,1,22,43,862,2122,40012,111859,2016566,6130494,106709364,
%T A305609 344744574,5831760108,19744810932,326100935448,1146472029123,
%U A305609 18549990711078,67282629958006,1069313429135204,3982410828494666,62297616737399876,237367322452180556
%N A305609 Expansion of 1/2 * (((1 + 8*x)/(1 - 8*x))^(1/8) - 1).
%C A305609 Let 1/2 * (((1 + k*x)/(1 - k*x))^(m/k) - 1) = a(0) + a(1)*x + a(2)*x^2 + ...
%C A305609 Then n*a(n) = 2*m*a(n-1) + k^2*(n-2)*a(n-2) for n > 1.
%C A305609 From _Peter Bala_, Sep 18 2026: (Start)
%C A305609 a(n) is odd iff n is a power of 2.
%C A305609 Proof. It is straightforward to check that the g.f. A(x) satisfies A + 7*A^2 + 28*A^3 + 70*A^4 + 112*A^5 + 112*A^6 + 64*A^7 + 16*A^8 = x/(1 - 8*x). Reducing this identity modulo 2 yields A == x + A^2 (mod 2), which iterates to A == x + x^2 + x^4 + x^8 + ... (mod 2), since for any g.f. F(x) we have F(x)^2 == F(x^2) (mod 2). Hence a(n) is 1 (mod 2) precisely for n a power of 2. (End)
%H A305609 Seiichi Manyama, <a href="/A305609/b305609.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..1000</a>
%F A305609 n*a(n) = 2*a(n-1) + 64*(n-2)*a(n-2) for n > 1.
%F A305609 a(n) = A303538(n)/2 for n > 0.
%F A305609 The g.f. A(x) satisfies 2*A(x)*(A(x) + 1) = 1/2 * (((1 + 8*x)/(1 - 8*x))^(1/4) - 1) and 2*A(x)*(A(x) + 1)*(2*A(x)^2 + 2*A(x) + 1) = 1/2 * (((1 + 8*x)/(1 - 8*x))^(1/2) - 1). - _Peter Bala_, Sep 19 2026
%p A305609 seq(coeff(series((1/2)*(((1+8*x)/(1-8*x))^(1/8)-1), x,30),x,n),n=0..25); # _Muniru A Asiru_, Jun 06 2018
%Y A305609 1/2 * (((1 + k*x)/(1 - k*x))^(1/k) - 1): A001405(n-1) (k=2), A305608 (k=4), this sequence (k=8).
